{
  "dom_id": "step-q101918",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The value $x=4$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\nI. $x-3>0$\n\nII. $2x\\le 10$\n\nIII. $1<x+5<6$",
  "worked_solution": "We substitute the given value into each of the inequalities and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=4$ into the first inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & >0 \\\\ 4-3 & >0 \\\\ 1 & >0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=4$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=4$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=4$ into the second inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot x & \\le 10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 4 & \\le 10 \\\\ 8 & \\le 10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=4$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=4$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value $x=4$ into the third inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1<x+5 & <6 \\\\ 1<4+5 & <6 \\\\ 1<9 & <6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=4$ leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=4$ ** does not satisfy** this inequality.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
